# THE APPLIANCE-FACING CENTRAL ENDPOINT — ONE VERSIONED PLACE, BOTH SIDES.
#
# Every appliance dials this name for the rest of its life, and every offline activation package carries it
# to appliances that have never been online. Central reads the same file to decide what to write into those
# packages, so the two can never drift apart.
#
# WHY A NAME AND NEVER AN ADDRESS
#
# Whatever is written here is baked into hotels. An IP would work on the day it was typed and strand the
# fleet the day Central moves: someone would have to visit every property. A name means a Central move is a
# DNS change plus one edit to this file, and nothing else. That is the entire reason this file exists rather
# than an environment variable set by hand on one server.
#
# An /etc/hosts entry on an appliance is a stopgap for a lab or for the hours before DNS propagates. It is
# never the product's dependency: it lives on exactly one machine and is invisible to the next deployment.
# Provisioning says so out loud when it finds one.
#
# HOW TO MOVE CENTRAL
#
#   1. Point the DNS record at the new host.
#   2. Change nothing here — the name has not changed.
#
# Only if the NAME itself changes does this file change, and then existing appliances must be reconfigured
# deliberately (they already trust the old name); that is a fleet migration, not a deployment step.
#
# OVERRIDING
#
# An environment variable of the same name wins, for staging and lab installs:
#
#   CENTRAL_BASE=https://central.lab.internal bash deploy/scripts/provision-fresh-appliance.sh
#
# There is no fallback in code. If neither this file nor the environment supplies a value, provisioning
# stops rather than inventing an endpoint that resolves to nothing.

# What an appliance dials for enrolment, licensing, assignment and reconciliation. Always outbound.
CENTRAL_BASE=https://sc-central.echofusion.com

# The mutual-TLS surface, same name, separate port. Split out so it can move to its own name later without
# touching appliances that already have a value pinned.
#
# 9443 IS THE PRODUCT'S PORT, not a choice made here: it is ctrlapi's CTRLAPI_MTLS_ADDR default, and it is
# what the live Central listens on. This file said 8443 and so did appliance provisioning — a port nothing
# has ever listened on. Nothing failed loudly, because the appliance only dials it for certificate
# enrolment; it simply would not have worked the first time it mattered.
CENTRAL_MTLS_BASE=https://sc-central.echofusion.com:9443

# What Central writes into offline activation packages. It is deliberately the appliance-facing name and NOT
# the human admin URL: the admin UI can later sit behind MFA, a VPN or Zero-Trust without any of that
# becoming a runtime dependency of a hotel's connectivity.
CTRLAPI_APPLIANCE_BASE=https://sc-central.echofusion.com
